Sure. Think of curl and wget as command-line tools that help you talk to websites or download files.

1. Real-life example of curl

Imagine you are working as a DevOps engineer and want to check whether your company's website is working.

You can run:

curl https://example.com

This sends a request to the website and prints the response.

For example:

$ curl https://example.com

<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Example Domain</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Example Domain</h1>
</body>
</html>

A more practical example: checking an API

Suppose your application has this API:

curl https://api.example.com/health

The server might return:

{
  "status": "healthy",
  "database": "connected"
}

In simple words:

    curl = "Go to this URL and show me what the server says."

You can also see HTTP details using:

curl -I https://example.com

Example:

HTTP/2 200
content-type: text/html
server: nginx
content-length: 1256

Here, 200 means the request was successful.
2. Real-life example of wget

Now imagine your manager gives you a 500 MB application package:

https://downloads.example.com/app.tar.gz

You want to download it to your Linux server.

Use:

wget https://downloads.example.com/app.tar.gz

You might see:

--2026-09-01 12:15:20--  https://downloads.example.com/app.tar.gz
Resolving downloads.example.com... 192.0.2.10
Connecting to downloads.example.com|192.0.2.10|:443... connected.
HTTP request sent, awaiting response... 200 OK
Length: 524288000 (500M) [application/gzip]
Saving to: 'app.tar.gz'

app.tar.gz        100%[===================>] 500.00M  25.4MB/s    in 20s

2026-09-01 12:15:40 (25.4 MB/s) - 'app.tar.gz' saved [524288000/524288000]

In simple words:

    wget = "Go to this URL and download this file for me."

3. Sample real-life server log

Suppose you have a web server running Nginx.

A log file might look like this:

192.168.1.10 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:20:01 +0530] "GET / HTTP/2.0" 200 1256
192.168.1.10 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:20:05 +0530] "GET /login HTTP/2.0" 200 3421
192.168.1.20 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:20:10 +0530] "GET /app.tar.gz HTTP/1.1" 200 524288000
192.168.1.30 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:20:15 +0530] "GET /missing.html HTTP/1.1" 404 153
192.168.1.40 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:20:20 +0530] "GET /api/health HTTP/1.1" 200 28

Let's understand one line:

192.168.1.10 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:20:01 +0530] "GET / HTTP/2.0" 200 1256

It basically means:

192.168.1.10       → Client IP
01/Sep/2026...     → Date and time
GET /              → Client requested the home page
HTTP/2.0           → HTTP protocol
200                → Request successful
1256               → Response size in bytes

4. curl vs wget — easiest way to remember

Situation	                     Use
Test an API	                     curl
Check HTTP response	             curl
Send POST request	             curl
Download one file	             wget
Download large files	         wget
Resume a download	             wget -c
Download recursively	         wget

5. Start with curl -v

The -v means verbose — show me what is happening behind the scenes.

curl -v https://example.com

You may see something like:

* Host example.com:443 was resolved.
* IPv4: 93.184.216.34
*   Trying 93.184.216.34:443...
* Connected to example.com (93.184.216.34) port 443
* SSL connection using TLSv1.3
> GET / HTTP/2
> Host: example.com
> User-Agent: curl/8.5.0
> Accept: */*
>
< HTTP/2 200
< content-type: text/html
< content-length: 1256
<
<!doctype html>
<html>
...

Don't worry about every line.

The important part is:

> GET /

This is the request sent by curl.

And:

< HTTP/2 200

This is the response from the server.

Think of it like a conversation:

curl → Server
       "Give me /"

Server → curl
         "Sure. Here is /"
         "Status: 200"

6. Understanding HTTP status codes

These are extremely important in DevOps.
200 — Success ✅

HTTP/2 200

Means:

    Everything worked.

Example:

curl https://myserver.com/api/health

Response:

{
  "status": "healthy"
}

Log:

10.0.0.15 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:30:01 +0530] "GET /api/health HTTP/1.1" 200 28

a) 301 — Permanently redirected

Suppose someone visits:

http://example.com

but the website wants everyone to use HTTPS.

The server might respond:

HTTP/1.1 301 Moved Permanently
Location: https://example.com/

You can investigate with:

curl -I http://example.com

You might get:

HTTP/1.1 301 Moved Permanently
Location: https://example.com/

Basically:

    "Don't use this address. Go over there instead."

b) 302 — Temporary redirect

Similar idea:

HTTP/1.1 302 Found
Location: /login

For example, you visit:

/dashboard

but you're not logged in.

The server might send you to:

/login

c) 404 — Not Found ❌

You run:

curl https://example.com/abc123

And get:

HTTP/2 404

This means:

    The server is working, but that particular resource doesn't exist.

Your log might contain:

10.0.0.20 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:35:10 +0530] "GET /abc123 HTTP/1.1" 404 153

As a DevOps engineer, you'd immediately notice the 404.

For example:

curl https://myserver.com/api/payment

Response:

HTTP/1.1 500 Internal Server Error

7. A realistic troubleshooting example

a) Imagine your customer says:

    "The website is down."

You don't immediately restart the server.

First:

curl -I https://mycompany.com

You get:

HTTP/2 200

Website is responding.

b) Then customer says:

    "Login isn't working."

You test:

curl -I https://mycompany.com/login

You get:

HTTP/2 200

So /login itself is reachable.

c) Now you check the application API:

curl -I https://mycompany.com/api/login

You get:

HTTP/2 500

8. Now look at the log

You open the access log:

tail -f /var/log/nginx/access.log

tail -f means:

    Show me the end of the file and keep watching for new lines.

You might see:

10.0.0.15 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:40:01 +0530] "GET / HTTP/2.0" 200 1256
10.0.0.15 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:40:03 +0530] "GET /login HTTP/2.0" 200 3421
10.0.0.15 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:40:05 +0530] "POST /api/login HTTP/2.0" 500 721
10.0.0.15 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:40:06 +0530] "GET /favicon.ico HTTP/2.0" 404 153

This tells you:

/                  → 200 ✅
/login             → 200 ✅
/api/login         → 500 ❌
/favicon.ico       → 404 ⚠️

The important problem is probably:

POST /api/login → 500

9. Access log vs error log

This distinction is very important.

a) Access log

Usually tells you:

    Who requested what, when, and what status they got.

Example:

10.0.0.15 - - [01/Sep/2026:12:40:05 +0530] \
"POST /api/login HTTP/2.0" 500 721

b) Error log

Usually tells you:

    What went wrong on the server.

For example:

2026/09/01 12:40:05 [error] 1234#1234:
*567 upstream prematurely closed connection
while reading response header from upstream,
client: 10.0.0.15,
request: "POST /api/login HTTP/2.0",
upstream: "http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/login"

10. Useful commands to remember:

a) Watch an Nginx log live

tail -f /var/log/nginx/access.log

b) Look at the last 50 log lines

tail -50 /var/log/nginx/access.log

c) Search for errors

grep "500" /var/log/nginx/access.log

d) Download a file

wget https://example.com/file.zip

e) Follow redirects with curl

curl -L https://example.com

f) Resume a partially downloaded file with wget

wget -c https://example.com/bigfile.zip

g) Test a website

curl https://example.com

h) Check only HTTP headers

curl -I https://example.com

i) See detailed connection information

curl -v https://example.com
